# Express.js API Rules — GoodEpoch Cursor Rules Pack v1
# Applies to: Express.js 5.x on Node.js 22+
# Stack: Express 5, TypeScript 5.5+, Node.js 22 LTS

## Project Structure

```
src/
  routes/           # Route handlers only — thin, delegate to services
  services/         # Business logic
  middleware/       # Custom middleware
  validators/       # Zod schemas for request validation
  models/           # Database models/types
  lib/              # DB client, external service clients
  types/            # Shared TypeScript types
  app.ts            # Express app setup (no server listen)
  server.ts         # Server entry point (listen here)
```

## Express 5 Specifics

- Express 5 natively supports async route handlers — no `express-async-errors` needed:
  ```typescript
  router.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
    const user = await userService.findById(req.params.id)
    res.json(user) // async errors auto-propagate to error handler
  })
  ```
- Use `Router` instances for feature modules, mount at app level
- `router.param()` for parameter preprocessing and validation

## Request Validation

- Validate ALL request inputs with zod before processing:
  ```typescript
  // validators/user.ts
  export const createUserSchema = z.object({
    body: z.object({
      email: z.string().email(),
      name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
    })
  })
  
  // middleware/validate.ts
  export const validate = (schema: AnyZodObject) =>
    async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
      const result = await schema.safeParseAsync({
        body: req.body, query: req.query, params: req.params
      })
      if (!result.success) {
        return res.status(400).json({ errors: result.error.flatten() })
      }
      next()
    }
  ```
- Apply validation middleware before route handler, not inside it

## Response Conventions

- Consistent response shape across all endpoints:
  ```typescript
  // Success
  res.status(200).json({ data: result })
  // Created
  res.status(201).json({ data: created, message: 'Created successfully' })
  // No content
  res.status(204).send()
  // Error
  res.status(400).json({ error: { code: 'VALIDATION_ERROR', message: '...', details: {} } })
  ```
- Never mix response shapes — define a typed `ApiResponse<T>` helper
- Set explicit status codes always — never rely on default 200

## Error Handling

- Centralized error handler as the last middleware:
  ```typescript
  app.use((err: Error, req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
    if (err instanceof AppError) {
      return res.status(err.statusCode).json({ error: err.toJSON() })
    }
    logger.error({ err, path: req.path }, 'Unhandled error')
    res.status(500).json({ error: { code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR', message: 'Internal server error' } })
  })
  ```
- Custom `AppError` class with `statusCode`, `code`, and `isOperational` fields
- Distinguish operational errors (user error, expected) from programmer errors (bugs)
- Never leak stack traces to API responses in production

## Middleware Stack (order matters)

```typescript
app.use(helmet())                    // Security headers
app.use(cors(corsConfig))            // CORS before routes
app.use(express.json({ limit: '1mb' }))
app.use(requestId())                 // Attach request ID
app.use(requestLogger())             // Log all requests
// Routes here
app.use(notFoundHandler)             // 404 catcher
app.use(errorHandler)                // Error handler last
```

## Authentication Middleware

- JWT verification as middleware, not per-route:
  ```typescript
  export const requireAuth: RequestHandler = async (req, res, next) => {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.replace('Bearer ', '')
    if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: { code: 'UNAUTHORIZED' } })
    try {
      req.user = await verifyToken(token)
      next()
    } catch {
      res.status(401).json({ error: { code: 'INVALID_TOKEN' } })
    }
  }
  ```
- Extend `Request` type via declaration merging in `types/express.d.ts`
- Use route-level middleware, not app-level, for auth (public routes exist)

## Service Layer Pattern

- Route handlers delegate to service functions. Zero business logic in routes:
  ```typescript
  // routes/users.ts
  router.post('/', validate(createUserSchema), async (req, res) => {
    const user = await userService.create(req.body)
    res.status(201).json({ data: user })
  })
  
  // services/user.ts
  export const userService = {
    create: async (input: CreateUserInput): Promise<User> => {
      // business logic, database calls here
    }
  }
  ```

## Logging

- Use `pino` for structured JSON logging — never `console.log` in production
- Log at request level with `pino-http`, include `requestId`, method, path, status, duration
- Log errors with full context: `logger.error({ err, userId, action }, 'message')`

## Environment and Config

- Validate env vars at startup, fail fast if required vars are missing:
  ```typescript
  // config/env.ts
  const envSchema = z.object({
    NODE_ENV: z.enum(['development', 'test', 'production']),
    PORT: z.coerce.number().default(3000),
    DATABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
    JWT_SECRET: z.string().min(32),
  })
  export const env = envSchema.parse(process.env)
  ```

## Performance and Security

- Use `compression` middleware for responses > 1KB
- Rate limiting with `express-rate-limit` on auth routes minimum
- `helmet()` for security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options)
- Connection pooling for database — never new connection per request
- Set `trust proxy` if behind reverse proxy: `app.set('trust proxy', 1)`
